\subsection{SDF Auxiliary Head}
\label{sec:sdf_head}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

\paragraph{Architecture.} The SDF head is a two-layer multilayer perceptron (MLP) that maps the action expert's internal features to per-timestep signed distance predictions. In $\pi_{0.5}$, the action expert is a Gemma-300M transformer that processes noisy action tokens alongside vision-language context. Let $f_t \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times H \times D}$ denote the action expert's output features (with $D = 1024$ for the Gemma-300M variant), which are normally projected to action-space dimension $D_a = 32$ by a linear output layer. We attach the SDF head to these features via a forward hook on the output projection layer, requiring no modification to the base model's source code. The head is defined as
\begin{equation}
\hat{s} = W_2 \, \mathrm{ReLU}(W_1 \, f_t + b_1) + b_2,
\end{equation}
where $W_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{256 \times 1024}$, $W_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 256}$, and $\hat{s} \in \mathbb{R}^{B \times H}$ gives the predicted minimum signed distance for each timestep in the action chunk, with the head applied independently to each of the $H$ timestep positions. The head contains approximately 263K parameters, which is negligible compared to the 300M-parameter action expert.

\paragraph{Ground-truth SDF computation.} Per-timestep signed distances are precomputed offline using MuJoCo's \texttt{mj\_geomDistance}, measuring minimum distance between end-effector geometries and scene obstacles (excluding grasped objects). Labels are stored as compressed arrays and aligned to action chunks at training time. Implementation details are in Appendix~\ref{app:sdf_labels}.

%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
\subsection{Noise-Aware Collision-Shaping Loss}
\label{sec:loss}
%-----------------------------------------------------------------------------

A direct collision penalty on the SDF predictions would be problematic in the flow-matching setting: at high noise levels (large $t$), the noisy actions $x_t$ bear little resemblance to the clean trajectory, and the model's internal features primarily reflect noise rather than meaningful geometric structure. Penalizing collisions uniformly across all noise levels would inject noisy gradients that destabilize training. We address this with a noise-aware weighting scheme that activates the collision penalty only when the model operates on near-clean inputs.

\paragraph{Noise-aware weight.} We define a time-dependent weight
\begin{equation}
w(t) = \sigma\!\left(\alpha \, (t_0 - t)\right),
\end{equation}
where $\sigma$ is the sigmoid function, $t_0 = 0.5$ is the transition center, and $\alpha = 10$ controls the sharpness. When $t \ll t_0$ (low noise), $w(t) \approx 1$ and the collision penalty is fully active. When $t \gg t_0$ (high noise), $w(t) \approx 0$ and the penalty is suppressed. The sigmoid provides a smooth transition that avoids discontinuities in the loss landscape (Figure~\ref{fig:weight_curve}).

\paragraph{SDF regression loss.} The first auxiliary loss encourages the SDF head to accurately predict signed distances from the model's internal features:
\begin{equation}
\mathcal{L}_{\mathrm{SDF}} = \frac{1}{|\mathcal{V}|} \sum_{(i,j) \in \mathcal{V}} \left(\hat{s}_{i,j} - s_{i,j}\right)^2,
\end{equation}
where $\mathcal{V}$ is the set of valid sample-timestep pairs with available ground-truth labels, $\hat{s}_{i,j}$ is the predicted signed distance, and $s_{i,j}$ is the ground truth. This loss ensures the auxiliary head provides a faithful geometric probe of the model's representations, which in turn supplies meaningful gradients for the collision penalty.

